[00:00] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: World Labs Marble UI showing an "Industrial Robot Training Facility" environment.
AUDIO: 'This entire 3D environment was generated from a single image using a new Claude skill called Image Blast from World Labs. If you\'ve never heard of World Labs before, they\'re the creators of Marble, which is an incredible world model that will take an image, turn it'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into a 360 panoramic, and then turn that into a Gaussian splat. Now, the difference with Image Blast is that instead of getting one gigantic Gaussian splat for the entire environment, Image Blast is actually going to use Claude'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and a bunch of other AI models to separate certain key elements from the environment. So, what you\'ll end up with is a sort of clean Gaussian splat of the bigger environment, but then detailed individual meshes that you can then move'

[00:37] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Claude UI with "Back at it, Matt Workman" and a prompt field.
AUDIO: 'around again in DCCs like Blender, Unreal Engine, etc. So, this is a major advancement as far as using AI to generate usable 3D almost production-ready environments for games, films, etc. Let\'s take a look at the'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'workflow. To use Image Blast, you\'re going to need three things. The first is Claude code from Anthropic. The second is a World Labs account where you\'re going to go create your own API key and save that. And third is a fall.ai'

[01:04] [scene:terminal]
VISUAL: Terminal showing `git clone https://github.com/neilsonnn/image-blaster`.
AUDIO: 'account. Again, you\'re going to need to create an API key for that as well. Now, I\'ll link to it in the description below, but you can look at the Image Blast GitHub. It has really clear and simple instructions for installation,'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which I followed. It went great. But, I\'ll walk you through how the process went from a high level as well. So, now to get started actually using Image Blast, go ahead and make a folder for your Claude project. You can call it Image Blast if you want. I just put it'

[01:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on my desktop. You\'re going to navigate to it and then use this Git Clone command to actually get it installed. Look at the GitHub for Image Blaster for the rest of the instructions. This is pretty much all I did, though. The next step and the most fun step is to make an'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'image of the environment that you want to create and turn into a 3D world. This could be a real photo, or of course it could be AI generated. For this demo, I went to fall.ai and used GPT Images 2.0 to generate an image of a brutalist'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'environment with linear warm lighting on the floor and skylight from above that\'s being used to train robots. So, it added a couple robots in there. Hit generate, I got four different'

[02:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'versions and this was the one that I chose to go with. So, now that you have an image of your environment, make sure to rename it something that\'s easy to reference later and take that image and put it in the inputs folder of your Claude project. Then head back to Claude'

[02:20] [scene:person]
VISUAL: Matt Workman speaking with code snippets overlaid, showing Claude's interaction.
AUDIO: 'and ask Claude to blast that image and the whole process is going to be automated and kicked off for you. So, while Claude\'s cooking, you will have to, of course, give it some guidance. It\'s usually just asking for right'

[02:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'permission and maybe something needs to get installed, but the whole process is very automated and you just get to sit back and watch Claude cook. So, what\'s happening in the background here is this skill is using a bunch of calls to the'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Fall API and those are specifically NanoBanana, which is going to analyze the environments. I don\'t know how it does that. It\'s going to pick out certain key features of it and then it\'s'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'going to separate those images of each little environment piece. In this case, it\'s a robot, it\'s a piece of the wall, it\'s one of the flags and it\'s going to kick off automatically multiple calls to the Fall API to start generating those'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'images by themselves. At the same time, once it\'s selected those kind of key objects, it\'s going to remove those images from the original image, basically making a clean plate. So, this is all an automated 2D process that'

[03:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'normally would take quite a bit of work in Photoshop or Nuke or something like that, but it\'s again is being completely automated by Claude and Fall, essentially Fall API. And then after that, it\'s going to take all of those images and then send those off to be'

[03:35] [scene:person]
VISUAL: Matt Workman speaking.
AUDIO: 'turned into 3D models, the Han Won 3D Tencent, I believe, image to 3D model. That is again completely automated. It\'s going to go create all of those into 3D models. And then the final step that is Well Labs specialty in being a world'

[03:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'world model is it\'s going to take the clean panoramic that\'s been created and turn that into an actual Gaussian splat. And then for people working in 3D, the Gaussian splat gets turned into a mesh. Now, one major caveat when it comes to'

[04:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this process that I want to point out that I experienced is that if you don\'t have a pro account over at World Labs is that it\'s going to be defaulting to the sort of standard, what I would call like low-quality preview version of the mesh'

